#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069637} :activity/id #uuid "a5eebdd0-457e-5351-b479-fdec034c955a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049227 :topic/id #uuid "e5039159-d776-5d2a-8b00-bb798bc20935" :topic/math-academy-id 446} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070036 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the integral  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2} \\textrm{d}x.$ Note that we have the repeated factor $(x+1)$ in the denominator. \n\nThe correct form of the partial fraction expansion of the integrand is\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2} \\,.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =A(x+1)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+2 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+2 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 2 & =A+B \\\\ 2 & =A-1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}= \\dfrac{3}{(x+1)} -\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nNow this function can be integrated term by term. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}|x+1|+\\frac{1}{x+1}+C\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant of integration." :tutorial/id #uuid "de271889-71f4-5fa2-8346-7ed8fa03e000" :tutorial/math-academy-id 922 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3ab3699-46bb-5348-b28d-d90ed53084d6" :step/math-academy-id 1800 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069644 :step/content {:db/id #e